I now have sufficient evidence to issue a comprehensive, independently verified review. Here is my verdict:

```json
{
  "verdict": "revise",
  "scores": {
    "novelty": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "The combination of BEq consensus + iterative expert iteration + oracle-freedom is genuinely unexplored, but it is a straightforward assembly of existing components (BEq partitioning from StepFun, expert iteration from ATLAS, consensus-trap mitigations from CoVerRL). The consensus collapse analysis adds modest novelty, but the mitigations themselves (KL regularization, temperature resampling) are standard techniques."
    },
    "feasibility": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "All components exist (StepFun-Formalizer-32B weights on HuggingFace, Kimina Lean Server, NuminaMath), but three critical unknowns are unaddressed: (1) BEq has 100% precision but extremely low recall due to prover brittleness—the ≥3 member filter may yield very small training sets; (2) no estimate of filter pass rate across 10K statements; (3) the 12–14 week timeline doesn't fit AAAI 2027 (Jul 28, 8 weeks away) or EACL ARR (Aug 3, 10 weeks), making only ICLR 2027 realistic."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Excellently written revision. Honest novelty positioning ('not first BEq consensus or first expert iteration'), detailed ablation plan with 6 baselines, transparent acknowledgment of consensus collapse risk, and clean separation of GPU/CPU costs. All Round 1 issues were addressed substantively."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "Oracle-freedom's practical value is questionable: LLM judges cost ~$50–100/round vs. $6,000+ in GPU costs, so removing them saves <2% of total cost. The theoretical motivation (avoiding convergence to oracle's knowledge center per Gerstgrasser) is interesting but may not translate to measurable gains over ATLAS-style LLM-rated expert iteration. The method may not advance absolute SOTA given ATF-32B's 94.51% Pass@1 on FormalMATH-Lite."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "IMPORTANT: BEq has 100% precision but extremely low recall due to prover brittleness (ICLR 2025, Poiroux et al.). The ≥3 member equivalence class threshold combined with low recall will systematically (a) shrink the effective training set per round (many correct formalizations won't be grouped), and (b) bias toward simpler, stylistically uniform formalizations where exact? can easily prove equivalence. This recall limitation and its downstream effects on training data quantity/diversity must be analyzed and addressed.",
    "No estimate of the BEq filter pass rate: What fraction of 10K NL statements will yield ≥3 BEq-consistent compilable candidates from k=16 samples? StepFun-Formalizer reports 40.5% BEq@1 at the end of training, implying that ~60% of statements don't even get one correct formalization. Early-round pass rates will be lower. If only 10–20% of statements pass, the effective training set is 1–2K pairs per round—potentially too small for meaningful SFT improvement on a 32B model.",
    "Consensus collapse mitigations are more monitoring than intervention: Of the four mitigations, (b) diversity entropy monitoring and (d) NLI cross-checks are explicitly labeled as monitoring/observational. The actual corrective interventions are (a) KL regularization (standard, not novel) and (c) temperature resampling on drift (a heuristic). A stronger, more principled mitigation is needed, e.g., periodically injecting known-correct BEq pairs as calibration anchors, or the generator-verifier co-evolution from CoVerRL adapted to the BEq setting.",
    "Oracle-freedom lacks compelling practical justification: The Gerstgrasser convergence-to-oracle argument is theoretical. Practically, the LLM oracle cost ($50–100/round for DeepSeek-V3 rating) is negligible vs. GPU costs ($6,000+). The paper needs to demonstrate a concrete, measurable advantage of oracle-freedom beyond cost—e.g., that BEq consensus produces more formally rigorous training signal than LLM ratings, or that oracle-dependent methods plateau while oracle-free methods continue improving.",
    "Timeline misalignment with primary venues: The 12–14 week manual timeline starting May 28 yields completion around August 27–September 10. AAAI 2027 (Jul 28) and EACL ARR (Aug 3) are clearly infeasible. Only ICLR 2027 (~Sep/Oct 2026, unannounced) is realistic. The venue list should be updated accordingly."
  ],
  "feedback": "The revision successfully addressed all Round 1 issues—ATLAS is properly cited, StepFun's BEq usage is correctly characterized, consensus collapse is analyzed with mitigations, novelty is honestly repositioned, and costs are separated. However, five issues prevent passing: (1) Add analysis of BEq's low recall and its impact on training data quantity/diversity under the ≥3 member threshold—consider using BEq+ (which combines more complex policy combinations) or lowering the threshold with compensating quality checks; (2) Estimate the filter pass rate empirically or analytically and discuss what happens if effective training data is too small (data augmentation strategies, curriculum over difficulty levels); (3) Strengthen consensus collapse mitigations from monitoring to intervention—adapt CoVerRL's generator-verifier co-evolution to the BEq setting rather than using generic KL regularization; (4) Justify oracle-freedom with a concrete, testable hypothesis beyond cost savings (e.g., 'BEq consensus produces training signal with higher formal fidelity than LLM ratings, leading to lower false-positive formalization rates in downstream theorem proving'); (5) Drop AAAI 2027 and EACL 2027 from target venues—the timeline only fits ICLR 2027.",
  "reason": "Well-revised idea with all Round 1 issues resolved, but average score (3.25) remains below 4.0 threshold due to unaddressed BEq recall limitations affecting training data yield, thin consensus collapse mitigations, and unclear practical advantage of oracle-freedom over cheap LLM judges."
}
```
